      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "A railroad reached Wahpeton in 1872, sparking rapid growth and turning the village into a bustling river trade hub.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        19,
        20,
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Wikipedia",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wahpeton%2C_North_Dakota",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Wahpeton sits where the Bois de Sioux and Otter Tail Rivers meet, forming the Red River of the North that flows north into Canada.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14,
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wahpeton%2C_North_Dakota",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The city is part of North Dakota’s most fertile agricultural region, producing soybeans, corn, wheat, and sugar beets.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.britannica.com/place/Wahpeton",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
